INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric technologist explains that ''ripstop'' woven fabric (discussed earlier as a periodic-reinforcement-thread technique) can ALSO be achieved via a WEAVE-STRUCTURE method: weaving a thicker yarn at regular intervals INTO the SAME weave pattern (rather than as a genuinely separate reinforcement system). Compared to the earlier periodic-reinforcement-thread approach, what practical MANUFACTURING difference does building the reinforcement directly into the weave pattern (using the loom''s existing warp/weft feed) offer?', '["Both approaches require mathematically identical manufacturing processes and equipment, making this comparison technically meaningless","Building the reinforcement grid directly into the standard weave pattern (using slightly thicker yarn fed at specific ends/picks within the SAME weaving process) avoids needing a SEPARATE reinforcement-yarn feed system/attachment on the loom, simplifying the manufacturing setup — this can be achieved through pattern/dobby programming alone on a standard loom, rather than requiring specialized additional hardware to insert a genuinely separate reinforcement thread system","Building reinforcement into the standard weave pattern always requires MORE specialized additional hardware than a separate reinforcement thread system, the reverse of the actual manufacturing-simplicity relationship between these two approaches","Ripstop fabric can only be achieved through the separate reinforcement-thread method, making the weave-structure-integrated approach described in this question technically impossible to actually manufacture"]'::jsonb, 'Building the reinforcement grid directly into the standard weave pattern (using slightly thicker yarn fed at specific ends/picks within the SAME weaving process) avoids needing a SEPARATE reinforcement-yarn feed system/attachment on the loom, simplifying the manufacturing setup — this can be achieved through pattern/dobby programming alone on a standard loom, rather than requiring specialized additional hardware to insert a genuinely separate reinforcement thread system', 'This extends the earlier ripstop-reinforcement discussion with a genuine manufacturing-method distinction — achieving the ripstop grid pattern by simply substituting a THICKER yarn at specific regular warp/weft positions WITHIN the standard weaving process (a dobby/pattern programming choice) avoids the need for any SEPARATE mechanical system to feed and insert a genuinely distinct reinforcement thread — this can be implemented on standard weaving equipment through pattern design alone, offering a simpler manufacturing setup than a dedicated separate reinforcement-thread insertion system, though the underlying functional principle (periodic strong points interrupting tear propagation) remains conceptually the same as the earlier discussion.', 3, '{"ripstop-weave-integration-method"}'::text[]),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric mill''s technologist explains ''fabric bow correction'' capability differs between finishing lines: some lines can only correct STRAIGHT-LINE skew, while advanced lines can correct CURVED bow patterns. Given the earlier bow-vs-skew distinction, why would a straight-line-only correction system FAIL to properly correct a genuinely curved/bowed fabric?', '["Straight-line correction systems can always fully correct any bow pattern with zero limitation, making this described capability distinction technically nonexistent","A skew defect is a CONSISTENT diagonal shift (correctable by applying a uniform diagonal counter-tension/stretch across the full width), while a BOW defect curves differently at different points across the width (e.g., more distortion in the center than at the edges) — a straight-line correction system applies the SAME uniform correction across the entire width, which can properly fix skew but would OVER-correct some areas and UNDER-correct others of a genuinely curved bow pattern, since bow requires a correction that itself VARIES continuously across the width to match the curve''s actual varying shape","Bow and skew defects are mathematically identical patterns, making any distinction in correction system capability between them technically irrelevant","Curved bow patterns can only be corrected through a completely different process (not fabric finishing at all), making any finishing-line-based correction system description technically inapplicable to bow defects specifically"]'::jsonb, 'A skew defect is a CONSISTENT diagonal shift (correctable by applying a uniform diagonal counter-tension/stretch across the full width), while a BOW defect curves differently at different points across the width (e.g., more distortion in the center than at the edges) — a straight-line correction system applies the SAME uniform correction across the entire width, which can properly fix skew but would OVER-correct some areas and UNDER-correct others of a genuinely curved bow pattern, since bow requires a correction that itself VARIES continuously across the width to match the curve''s actual varying shape', 'This directly extends the earlier bow-vs-skew distinction (established in the weaving domain) with a specific finishing-equipment-capability insight — since skew is a CONSISTENT diagonal shift (the same directional offset repeated uniformly across the entire width), a straight-line/uniform correction system can properly counteract it with a single uniform diagonal adjustment; but since BOW curves differently at DIFFERENT points across the width (varying distortion magnitude/direction from edge to center), a uniform straight-line correction would necessarily over-correct in some areas and under-correct in others — properly correcting a genuine bow requires a correction system capable of applying a CONTINUOUSLY VARYING adjustment across the width (matching the bow''s actual curved shape), which is precisely why advanced weft-straightening equipment (discussed earlier) specifically needs this curve-matching capability, not just uniform straight-line correction.', 3, '{"bow-correction-capability"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
